{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And Yosef harnessed his chariot.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory darshaned: Rabbi Yudan said, two men saw a glory that no creature had ever seen, and they are Yitro and Yaakov. When Yitro came to Moshe, what is written of him? (Exodus 18:7) ״ויצא משה לקראת חותנו״ — and Moshe went out to meet his father-in-law. Who saw Moshe going out — and he was not going out? Who saw Aharon going out — and he was not going out? Who saw the seventy elders going out — and he was not going out? It turns out that all of these were going out after Yitro. And likewise Yaakov: who saw Yosef going out to meet his father — and he was not going out? Who saw the elders of Pharaoh going out — and he was not going out? Who saw the entire camp of Egypt — and he was not going out? To fulfill what is said (Isaiah 24:23) ״ונגד זקניו כבוד״ — 'and before his elders, glory.'" "<b>And he fell upon his neck and wept upon his neck still more.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that one should not understand that Yosef fell upon his father's neck, for that would not be in accordance with proper conduct; rather, it would be fitting to kiss his hands or to bow down to him. Rather, everything returns to Yaakov, for Yaakov fell upon Yosef's neck and wept upon his neck still more, just as he had wept continually until now when he did not see him. And it is well known whose tear is more abundant — whether the old father who finds his son alive after despair and after mourning, or the young man whom they have made king over all the land of Egypt."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:50.335486535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}